  "slug": "development-reveals-5600",
  "summary": "Single-cell and cross-species multiomic atlases now resolve human-enriched cortical progenitor and enhancer programs at mid-gestation.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints converge on the developing human cortex, where regulatory and progenitor programs are being resolved at single-cell and cross-species resolution. Human-specific transposable elements are reported in cortical organoids to regulate the timing of the radial glia-to-neuron transition, with locus-resolved single-cell long-read profiling paired with CRISPRi-based manipulation. Cross-species multiomics of human, macaque and mouse fetal cortex identifies RHOJ as an outer radial glia-enriched regulator of human-enriched progenitor states. A single-nucleus chromatin accessibility atlas of 1.17 million candidate enhancers across 104,000 mid-gestation cortical nuclei provides a lineage-restricted regulatory reference. VEGABrain complements these resources as an open web tool that projects bulk RNA-seq and ssGSEA gene-set enrichment onto human brain atlas surfaces across developmental stages. The shared thread is the mapping of human-specific regulatory and progenitor states, while VEGABrain is a visualization resource rather than an experimental result.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
